and is used for the application logic of browser-based productivity\par
applications such as Google Mail, Google Docs and Zimbra Col-\par
laboration Suite. In this domain, in order to provide a fluid user\par
experience and enable a new generation of applications, virtual ma-\par
chines must provide a low startup time and high performance.\par
Compilers for statically typed languages rely on type informa-\par
tion to generate efficient machine code. In a dynamically typed pro-\par
gramming language such as JavaScript, the types of expressions\par
may vary at runtime. This means that the compiler can no longer\par
easily transform operations into machine instructions that operate\par
on one specific type. Without exact type information, the compiler\par
must emit slower generalized machine code that can deal with all\par
potential type combinations. While compile-time static type infer-\par
ence might be able to gather type information to generate opti-\par
mized machine code, traditional static analysis is very expensive\par
and hence not well suited for the highly interactive environment of\par
a web browser.\par
We present a trace-based compilation technique for dynamic\par
languages that reconciles speed of compilation with excellent per-\par
formance of the generated machine code. Our system uses a mixed-\par
mode execution approach: the system starts running JavaScript in a\par
fast-starting bytecode interpreter. As the program runs, the system\par
identifieshot(frequently executed) bytecode sequences, records\par
them, and compiles them to fast native code. We call such a se-\par
quence of instructions atrace.\par
Unlike method-based dynamic compilers, our dynamic com-\par
piler operates at the granularity of individual loops. This design\par
choice is based on the expectation that programs spend most of\par
their time in hot loops. Even in dynamically typed languages, we\par
expect hot loops to be mostlytype-stable, meaning that the types of\par
values are invariant. (12) For example, we would expect loop coun-\par
ters that start as integers to remain integers for all iterations. When\par
both of these expectations hold, a trace-based compiler can cover\par
the program execution with a small number of type-specialized, ef-\par
ficiently compiled traces.\par
Each compiled trace covers one path through the program with\par
one mapping of values to types. When the VM executes a compiled\par
trace, it cannot guarantee that the same path will be followed\par
or that the same types will occur in subsequent loop iterations.\par
\par
Hence, recording and compiling a tracespeculatesthat the path and\par
typing will be exactly as they were during recording for subsequent\par
iterations of the loop.\par
Every compiled trace contains all theguards(checks) required\par
to validate the speculation. If one of the guards fails (if control\par
flow is different, or a value of a different type is generated), the\par
trace exits. If an exit becomes hot, the VM can record abranch\par
tracestarting at the exit to cover the new path. In this way, the VM\par
records atrace treecovering all the hot paths through the loop.\par
Nested loops can be difficult to optimize for tracing VMs. In\par
a na\par
 ̈\par
ıve implementation, inner loops would become hot first, and\par
the VM would start tracing there. When the inner loop exits, the\par
VM would detect that a different branch was taken. The VM would\par
try to record a branch trace, and find that the trace reaches not the\par
inner loop header, but the outer loop header. At this point, the VM\par
could continue tracing until it reaches the inner loop header again,\par
thus tracing the outer loop inside a trace tree for the inner loop.\par
But this requires tracing a copy of the outer loop for every side exit\par
and type combination in the inner loop. In essence, this is a form\par
of unintended tail duplication, which can easily overflow the code\par
cache. Alternatively, the VM could simply stop tracing, and give up\par
on ever tracing outer loops.\par
We solve the nested loop problem by recordingnested trace\par
trees. Our system traces the inner loop exactly as the na\par
 ̈\par
ıve version.\par
The system stops extending the inner tree when it reaches an outer\par
loop, but then it starts a new trace at the outer loop header. When\par
the outer loop reaches the inner loop header, the system tries to call\par
the trace tree for the inner loop. If the call succeeds, the VM records\par
the call to the inner tree as part of the outer trace and finishes\par
the outer trace as normal. In this way, our system can trace any\par
number of loops nested to any depth without causing excessive tail\par
duplication.\par
These techniques allow a VM to dynamically translate a pro-\par
gram to nested, type-specialized trace trees. Because traces can\par
cross function call boundaries, our techniques also achieve the ef-\par
fects of inlining. Because traces have no internal control-flow joins,\par
they can be optimized in linear time by a simple compiler (10).\par
Thus, our tracing VM efficiently performs the same kind of op-\par
timizations that would require interprocedural analysis in a static\par
optimization setting. This makes tracing an attractive and effective\par
tool to type specialize even complex function call-rich code.\par
We implemented these techniques for an existing JavaScript in-\par
terpreter, SpiderMonkey. We call the resulting tracing VMTrace-\par
Monkey. TraceMonkey supports all the JavaScript features of Spi-\par
derMonkey, with a 2x-20x speedup for traceable programs.\par
This paper makes the following contributions:\par
•\par
We explain an algorithm for dynamically forming trace trees to\par
cover a program, representing nested loops as nested trace trees.\par
•\par
We explain how to speculatively generate efficient type-specialized\par
code for traces from dynamic language programs.\par
•\par
We validate our tracing techniques in an implementation based\par
on the SpiderMonkey JavaScript interpreter, achieving 2x-20x\par
speedups on many programs.\par
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 3 is\par
a general overview of trace tree based compilation we use to cap-\par
