2.  Overview: Example Tracing Run\par
This section provides an overview of our system by describing\par
how TraceMonkey executes an example program. The example\par
program, shown in Figure 1, computes the first 100 prime numbers\par
with nested loops. The narrative should be read along with Figure 2,\par
which describes the activities TraceMonkey performs and when it\par
transitions between the loops.\par
TraceMonkey always begins executing a program in the byte-\par
code interpreter. Every loop back edge is a potential trace point.\par
When the interpreter crosses a loop edge, TraceMonkey invokes\par
thetrace monitor, which may decide to record or execute a native\par
trace. At the start of execution, there are no compiled traces yet, so\par
the trace monitor counts the number of times each loop back edge is\par
executed until a loop becomeshot, currently after 2 crossings. Note\par
that the way our loops are compiled, the loop edge is crossed before\par
entering the loop, so the second crossing occurs immediately after\par
the first iteration.\par
