3.  Trace Trees\par
In this section, we describe traces, trace trees, and how they are\par
formed at run time. Although our techniques apply to any dynamic\par
language interpreter, we will describe them assuming a bytecode\par
interpreter to keep the exposition simple.\par
3.1  Traces\par
Atraceis simply a program path, which may cross function call\par
boundaries. TraceMonkey focuses onloop traces, that originate at\par
a loop edge and represent a single iteration through the associated\par
loop.\par
Similar to an extended basic block, a trace is only entered at\par
the top, but may have many exits. In contrast to an extended basic\par
block, a trace can contain join nodes. Since a trace always only\par
follows one single path through the original program, however, join\par
nodes are not recognizable as such in a trace and have a single\par
predecessor node like regular nodes.\par
Atyped traceis a trace annotated with a type for every variable\par
(including temporaries) on the trace. A typed trace also has an entry\par
type mapgiving the required types for variables used on the trace\par
before they are defined. For example, a trace could have a type map\par
(x: int, b: boolean), meaning that the trace may be entered\par
only if the value of the variablexis of typeintand the value ofb\par
is of typeboolean. The entry type map is much like the signature\par
of a function.\par
In this paper, we only discuss typed loop traces, and we will\par
refer to them simply as “traces”. The key property of typed loop\par
traces is that they can be compiled to efficient machine code using\par
the same techniques used for typed languages.\par
In TraceMonkey, traces are recorded in trace-flavored SSALIR\par
(low-level intermediate representation). In trace-flavored SSA (or\par
TSSA), phi nodes appear only at the entry point, which is reached\par
both on entry and via loop edges. The important LIR primitives\par
are constant values, memory loads and stores (by address and\par
offset), integer operators, floating-point operators, function calls,\par
and conditional exits. Type conversions, such as integer to double,\par
are represented by function calls. This makes the LIR used by\par
TraceMonkey independent of the concrete type system and type\par
conversion rules of the source language. The LIR operations are\par
generic enough that the backend compiler is language independent.\par
Figure 3 shows an example LIR trace.\par
Bytecode interpreters typically represent values in a various\par
complex data structures (e.g., hash tables) in a boxed format (i.e.,\par
with attached type tag bits). Since a trace is intended to represent\par
efficient code that eliminates all that complexity, our traces oper-\par
ate on unboxed values in simple variables and arrays as much as\par
possible.\par
A trace records all its intermediate values in a small activation\par
record area. To make variable accesses fast on trace, the trace also\par
imports local and global variables by unboxing them and copying\par
them to its activation record. Thus, the trace can read and write\par
these variables with simple loads and stores from a native activation\par
recording, independently of the boxing mechanism used by the\par
interpreter. When the trace exits, the VM boxes the values from\par
this native storage location and copies them back to the interpreter\par
structures.\par
For every control-flow branch in the source program, the\par
recorder generates conditional exit LIR instructions. These instruc-\par
tions exit from the trace if required control flow is different from\par
what it was at trace recording, ensuring that the trace instructions\par
are run only if they are supposed to. We call these instructions\par
guardinstructions.\par
Most of our traces represent loops and end with the specialloop\par
LIR instruction. This is just an unconditional branch to the top of\par
the trace. Such traces return only via guards.\par
Now, we describe the key optimizations that are performed as\par
part of recording LIR. All of these optimizations reduce complex\par
dynamic language constructs to simple typed constructs by spe-\par
cializing for the current trace. Each optimization requires guard in-\par
structions to verify their assumptions about the state and exit the\par
trace if necessary.\par
Type specialization.\par
All LIR primitives apply to operands of specific types. Thus,\par
LIR traces are necessarily type-specialized, and a compiler can\par
easily produce a translation that requires no type dispatches. A\par
typical bytecode interpreter carries tag bits along with each value,\par
and to perform any operation, must check the tag bits, dynamically\par
dispatch, mask out the tag bits to recover the untagged value,\par
perform the operation, and then reapply tags. LIR omits everything\par
except the operation itself.\par
A potential problem is that some operations can produce values\par
of unpredictable types. For example, reading a property from an\par
object could yield a value of any type, not necessarily the type\par
observed during recording. The recorder emits guard instructions\par
that conditionally exit if the operation yields a value of a different\par
type from that seen during recording. These guard instructions\par
guarantee that as long as execution is on trace, the types of values\par
match those of the typed trace. When the VM observes a side exit\par
along such a type guard, a new typed trace is recorded originating\par
at the side exit location, capturing the new type of the operation in\par
question.\par
Representation specialization: objects.In JavaScript, name\par
lookup semantics are complex and potentially expensive because\par
they include features like object inheritance andeval. To evaluate\par
an object property read expression likeo.x, the interpreter must\par
search the property map ofoand all of its prototypes and parents.\par
Property maps can be implemented with different data structures\par
(e.g., per-object hash tables or shared hash tables), so the search\par
process also must dispatch on the representation of each object\par
found during search. TraceMonkey can simply observe the result of\par
the search process and record the simplest possible LIR to access\par
the property value. For example, the search might finds the value of\par
o.xin the prototype ofo, which uses a shared hash-table represen-\par
tation that placesxin slot 2 of a property vector. Then the recorded\par
can generate LIR that readso.xwith just two or three loads: one to\par
get the prototype, possibly one to get the property value vector, and\par
one more to get slot 2 from the vector. This is a vast simplification\par
and speedup compared to the original interpreter code. Inheritance\par
relationships and object representations can change during execu-\par
tion, so the simplified code requires guard instructions that ensure\par
the object representation is the same. In TraceMonkey, objects’ rep-\par
\par
resentations are assigned an integer key called theobject shape.\par
Thus, the guard is a simple equality check on the object shape.\par
Representation specialization: numbers.JavaScript has no\par
integer type, only a Number type that is the set of 64-bit IEEE-\par
754 floating-pointer numbers (“doubles”). But many JavaScript\par
operators, in particular array accesses and bitwise operators, really\par
operate on integers, so they first convert the number to an integer,\par
and then convert any integer result back to a double.\par
1\par
Clearly, a\par
JavaScript VM that wants to be fast must find a way to operate on\par
integers directly and avoid these conversions.\par
In TraceMonkey, we support two representations for numbers:\par
integers and doubles. The interpreter uses integer representations\par
as much as it can, switching for results that can only be represented\par
as doubles. When a trace is started, some values may be imported\par
and represented as integers. Some operations on integers require\par
guards. For example, adding two integers can produce a value too\par
large for the integer representation.\par
Function inlining.LIR traces can cross function boundaries\par
in either direction, achieving function inlining. Move instructions\par
need to be recorded for function entry and exit to copy arguments\par
in and return values out. These move statements are then optimized\par
away by the compiler using copy propagation. In order to be able\par
to return to the interpreter, the trace must also generate LIR to\par
record that a call frame has been entered and exited. The frame\par
entry and exit LIR saves just enough information to allow the\par
intepreter call stack to be restored later and is much simpler than\par
the interpreter’s standard call code. If the function being entered\par
is not constant (which in JavaScript includes any call by function\par
name), the recorder must also emit LIR to guard that the function\par
is the same.\par
Guards and side exits.Each optimization described above\par
requires one or more guards to verify the assumptions made in\par
doing the optimization. A guard is just a group of LIR instructions\par
that performs a test and conditional exit. The exit branches to a\par
side exit, a small off-trace piece of LIR that returns a pointer to\par
a structure that describes the reason for the exit along with the\par
interpreter PC at the exit point and any other data needed to restore\par
the interpreter’s state structures.\par
Aborts.Some constructs are difficult to record in LIR traces.\par
For example,evalor calls to external functions can change the\par
program state in unpredictable ways, making it difficult for the\par
tracer to know the current type map in order to continue tracing.\par
A tracing implementation can also have any number of other limi-\par
tations, e.g.,a small-memory device may limit the length of traces.\par
When any situation occurs that prevents the implementation from\par
continuing trace recording, the implementationabortstrace record-\par
ing and returns to the trace monitor.\par
3.2  Trace Trees\par
Especially simple loops, namely those where control flow, value\par
types, value representations, and inlined functions are all invariant,\par
can be represented by a single trace. But most loops have at least\par
some variation, and so the program will take side exits from the\par
main trace. When a side exit becomes hot, TraceMonkey starts a\par
newbranch tracefrom that point and patches the side exit to jump\par
directly to that trace. In this way, a single trace expands on demand\par
to a single-entry, multiple-exittrace tree.\par
This section explains how trace trees are formed during execu-\par
tion. The goal is to form trace trees during execution that cover all\par
the hot paths of the program.\par
1\par
Arrays are actually worse than this: if the index value is a number, it must\par
be converted from a double to a string for the property access operator, and\par
then to an integer internally to the array implementation.\par
Starting a tree.Tree trees always start at loop headers, because\par
they are a natural place to look for hot paths. In TraceMonkey, loop\par
headers are easy to detect–the bytecode compiler ensures that a\par
bytecode is a loop header iff it is the target of a backward branch.\par
TraceMonkey starts a tree when a given loop header has been exe-\par
cuted a certain number of times (2 in the current implementation).\par
Starting a tree just means starting recording a trace for the current\par
point and type map and marking the trace as the root of a tree. Each\par
tree is associated with a loop header and type map, so there may be\par
several trees for a given loop header.\par
Closing the loop.Trace recording can end in several ways.\par
Ideally, the trace reaches the loop header where it started with\par
the same type map as on entry. This is called atype-stableloop\par
iteration. In this case, the end of the trace can jump right to the\par
beginning, as all the value representations are exactly as needed to\par
enter the trace. The jump can even skip the usual code that would\par
copy out the state at the end of the trace and copy it back in to the\par
trace activation record to enter a trace.\par
In certain cases the trace might reach the loop header with a\par
different type map. This scenario is sometime observed for the first\par
iteration of a loop. Some variables inside the loop might initially be\par
undefined, before they are set to a concrete type during the first loop\par
iteration. When recording such an iteration, the recorder cannot\par
link the trace back to its own loop header since it istype-unstable.\par
Instead, the iteration is terminated with a side exit that will always\par
fail and return to the interpreter. At the same time a new trace is\par
recorded with the new type map. Every time an additional type-\par
unstable trace is added to a region, its exit type map is compared to\par
the entry map of all existing traces in case they complement each\par
other. With this approach we are able to cover type-unstable loop\par
iterations as long they eventually form a stable equilibrium.\par
Finally, the trace might exit the loop before reaching the loop\par
header, for example because execution reaches abreakorreturn\par
statement. In this case, the VM simply ends the trace with an exit\par
to the trace monitor.\par
As mentioned previously, we may speculatively chose to rep-\par
resent certain Number-typed values as integers on trace. We do so\par
when we observe that Number-typed variables contain an integer\par
value at trace entry. If during trace recording the variable is unex-\par
pectedly assigned a non-integer value, we have to widen the type\par
of the variable to a double. As a result, the recorded trace becomes\par
inherently type-unstable since it starts with an integer value but\par
ends with a double value. This represents a mis-speculation, since\par
at trace entry we specialized the Number-typed value to an integer,\par
assuming that at the loop edge we would again find an integer value\par
in the variable, allowing us to close the loop. To avoid future spec-\par
ulative failures involving this variable, and to obtain a type-stable\par
trace we note the fact that the variable in question as been observed\par
to sometimes hold non-integer values in an advisory data structure\par
which we call the “oracle”.\par
When compiling loops, we consult the oracle before specializ-\par
ing values to integers. Speculation towards integers is performed\par
only if no adverse information is known to the oracle about that\par
particular variable. Whenever we accidentally compile a loop that\par
is type-unstable due to mis-speculation of a Number-typed vari-\par
able, we immediately trigger the recording of a new trace, which\par
based on the now updated oracle information will start with a dou-\par
ble value and thus become type stable.\par
Extending a tree.Side exits lead to different paths through\par
the loop, or paths with different types or representations. Thus, to\par
completely cover the loop, the VM must record traces starting at all\par
side exits. These traces are recorded much like root traces: there is\par
a counter for each side exit, and when the counter reaches a hotness\par
threshold, recording starts. Recording stops exactly as for the root\par
trace, using the loop header of the root trace as the target to reach.\par
\par
Our implementation does not extend at all side exits. It extends\par
only if the side exit is for a control-flow branch, and only if the side\par
exit does not leave the loop. In particular we do not want to extend\par
a trace tree along a path that leads to an outer loop, because we\par
want to cover such paths in an outer tree through treenesting.\par
3.3  Blacklisting\par
Sometimes, a program follows a path that cannot be compiled\par
into a trace, usually because of limitations in the implementation.\par
TraceMonkey does not currently support recording throwing and\par
catching of arbitrary exceptions. This design trade off was chosen,\par
because exceptions are usually rare in JavaScript. However, if a\par
program opts to use exceptions intensively, we would suddenly\par
incur a punishing runtime overhead if we repeatedly try to record\par
a trace for this path and repeatedly fail to do so, since we abort\par
tracing every time we observe an exception being thrown.\par
As a result, if a hot loop contains traces that always fail, the VM\par
could potentially run much more slowly than the base interpreter:\par
the VM repeatedly spends time trying to record traces, but is never\par
able to run any. To avoid this problem, whenever the VM is about\par
to start tracing, it must try to predict whether it will finish the trace.\par
Our prediction algorithm is based onblacklistingtraces that\par
have been tried and failed. When the VM fails to finish a trace start-\par
ing at a given point, the VM records that a failure has occurred. The\par
VM also sets a counter so that it will not try to record a trace starting\par
at that point until it is passed a few more times (32 in our imple-\par
mentation). Thisbackoffcounter gives temporary conditions that\par
prevent tracing a chance to end. For example, a loop may behave\par
differently during startup than during its steady-state execution. Af-\par
ter a given number of failures (2 in our implementation), the VM\par
marks the fragment as blacklisted, which means the VM will never\par
again start recording at that point.\par
After implementing this basic strategy, we observed that for\par
small loops that get blacklisted, the system can spend a noticeable\par
amount of time just finding the loop fragment and determining that\par
it has been blacklisted. We now avoid that problem by patching the\par
bytecode. We define an extra no-op bytecode that indicates a loop\par
header. The VM calls into the trace monitor every time the inter-\par
preter executes a loop header no-op. To blacklist a fragment, we\par
simply replace the loop header no-op with a regular no-op. Thus,\par
the interpreter will never again even call into the trace monitor.\par
There is a related problem we have not yet solved, which occurs\par
when a loop meets all of these conditions:\par
•\par
The VM can form at least one root trace for the loop.\par
•\par
There is at least one hot side exit for which the VM cannot\par
complete a trace.\par
•\par
The loop body is short.\par
In this case, the VM will repeatedly pass the loop header, search\par
for a trace, find it, execute it, and fall back to the interpreter.\par
With a short loop body, the overhead of finding and calling the\par
trace is high, and causes performance to be even slower than the\par
basic interpreter. So far, in this situation we have improved the\par
implementation so that the VM can complete the branch trace.\par
But it is hard to guarantee that this situation will never happen.\par
As future work, this situation could be avoided by detecting and\par
blacklisting loops for which the average trace call executes few\par
bytecodes before returning to the interpreter.\par
