a compiled trace for the outer tree, we simply exit the outer trace\par
and start recording a new branch in the inner tree.\par
4.2  Blacklisting with Nesting\par
The blacklisting algorithm needs modification to work well with\par
nesting. The problem is that outer loop traces often abort during\par
startup (because the inner tree is not available or takes a side exit),\par
which would lead to their being quickly blacklisted by the basic\par
algorithm.\par
The key observation is that when an outer trace aborts because\par
the inner tree is not ready, this is probably a temporary condition.\par
Thus, we should not count such aborts toward blacklisting as long\par
as we are able to build up more traces for the inner tree.\par
In our implementation, when an outer tree aborts on the inner\par
tree, we increment the outer tree’s blacklist counter as usual and\par
back off on compiling it. When the inner tree finishes a trace, we\par
decrement the blacklist counter on the outer loop, “forgiving” the\par
outer loop for aborting previously. We also undo the backoff so that\par
the outer tree can start immediately trying to compile the next time\par
we reach it.\par
5.  Trace Tree Optimization\par
This section explains how a recorded trace is translated to an\par
optimized machine code trace. The trace compilation subsystem,\par
NANOJIT, is separate from the VM and can be used for other\par
applications.\par
\par
5.1  Optimizations\par
Because traces are in SSA form and have no join points orφ-\par
nodes, certain optimizations are easy to implement. In order to\par
get good startup performance, the optimizations must run quickly,\par
so we chose a small set of optimizations. We implemented the\par
optimizations as pipelined filters so that they can be turned on and\par
off independently, and yet all run in just two loop passes over the\par
trace: one forward and one backward.\par
Every time the trace recorder emits a LIR instruction, the in-\par
struction is immediately passed to the first filter in the forward\par
pipeline. Thus, forward filter optimizations are performed as the\par
trace is recorded. Each filter may pass each instruction to the next\par
filter unchanged, write a different instruction to the next filter, or\par
write no instruction at all. For example, the constant folding filter\par
can replace a multiply instruction likev\par
13\par
:=mul3,1000with a\par
constant instructionv\par
13\par
= 3000.\par
We currently apply four forward filters:\par
•\par
On ISAs without floating-point instructions, a soft-float filter\par
converts floating-point LIR instructions to sequences of integer\par
instructions.\par
•\par
CSE (constant subexpression elimination),\par
•\par
expression simplification, including constant folding and a few\par
algebraic identities (e.g.,a−a= 0), and\par
•\par
source language semantic-specific expression simplification,\par
primarily algebraic identities that allowDOUBLEto be replaced\par
withINT. For example, LIR that converts anINTto aDOUBLE\par
and then back again would be removed by this filter.\par
When trace recording is completed, nanojit runs the backward\par
optimization filters. These are used for optimizations that require\par
backward program analysis. When running the backward filters,\par
nanojit reads one LIR instruction at a time, and the reads are passed\par
through the pipeline.\par
We currently apply three backward filters:\par
•\par
Dead data-stack store elimination. The LIR trace encodes many\par
stores to locations in the interpreter stack. But these values are\par
never read back before exiting the trace (by the interpreter or\par
another trace). Thus, stores to the stack that are overwritten\par
before the next exit are dead. Stores to locations that are off\par
the top of the interpreter stack at future exits are also dead.\par
•\par
Dead call-stack store elimination. This is the same optimization\par
as above, except applied to the interpreter’s call stack used for\par
function call inlining.\par
•\par
Dead code elimination. This eliminates any operation that\par
stores to a value that is never used.\par
After a LIR instruction is successfully read (“pulled”) from\par
the backward filter pipeline, nanojit’s code generator emits native\par
machine instruction(s) for it.\par
