6.  Implementation\par
To demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, we have im-\par
plemented a trace-based dynamic compiler for the SpiderMonkey\par
JavaScript Virtual Machine (4). SpiderMonkey is the JavaScript\par
VM embedded in Mozilla’s Firefox open-source web browser (2),\par
which is used by more than 200 million users world-wide. The core\par
of SpiderMonkey is a bytecode interpreter implemented in C++.\par
In SpiderMonkey, all JavaScript values are represented by the\par
typejsval. Ajsvalis machine word in which up to the 3 of the\par
least significant bits are a type tag, and the remaining bits are data.\par
See Figure 6 for details. All pointers contained injsvalspoint to\par
GC-controlled blocks aligned on 8-byte boundaries.\par
JavaScriptobjectvalues are mappings of string-valued property\par
names to arbitrary values. They are represented in one of two ways\par
in SpiderMonkey. Most objects are represented by a shared struc-\par
tural description, called theobject shape, that maps property names\par
to array indexes using a hash table. The object stores a pointer to\par
the shape and the array of its own property values. Objects with\par
large, unique sets of property names store their properties directly\par
in a hash table.\par
The garbage collector is an exact, non-generational, stop-the-\par
world mark-and-sweep collector.\par
In the rest of this section we discuss key areas of the TraceMon-\par
key implementation.\par
6.1  Calling Compiled Traces\par
Compiled traces are stored in atrace cache, indexed by intepreter\par
PC and type map. Traces are compiled so that they may be\par
called as functions using standard native calling conventions (e.g.,\par
FASTCALLon x86).\par
The interpreter must hit a loop edge and enter the monitor in\par
order to call a native trace for the first time. The monitor computes\par
the current type map, checks the trace cache for a trace for the\par
current PC and type map, and if it finds one, executes the trace.\par
To execute a trace, the monitor must build a trace activation\par
record containing imported local and global variables, temporary\par
stack space, and space for arguments to native calls. The local and\par
global values are then copied from the interpreter state to the trace\par
activation record. Then, the trace is called like a normal C function\par
pointer.\par
\par
When a trace call returns, the monitor restores the interpreter\par
state. First, the monitor checks the reason for the trace exit and\par
applies blacklisting if needed. Then, it pops or synthesizes inter-\par
preter JavaScript call stack frames as needed. Finally, it copies the\par
imported variables back from the trace activation record to the in-\par
terpreter state.\par
At least in the current implementation, these steps have a non-\par
negligible runtime cost, so minimizing the number of interpreter-\par
to-trace and trace-to-interpreter transitions is essential for perfor-\par
mance. (see also Section 3.3). Our experiments (see Figure 12)\par
show that for programs we can trace well such transitions hap-\par
pen infrequently and hence do not contribute significantly to total\par
runtime. In a few programs, where the system is prevented from\par
recording branch traces for hot side exits by aborts, this cost can\par
rise to up to 10% of total execution time.\par
6.2  Trace Stitching\par
Transitions from a trace to a branch trace at a side exit avoid the\par
costs of calling traces from the monitor, in a feature calledtrace\par
stitching. At a side exit, the exiting trace only needs to write live\par
register-carried values back to its trace activation record. In our im-\par
plementation, identical type maps yield identical activation record\par
layouts, so the trace activation record can be reused immediately\par
by the branch trace.\par
In programs with branchy trace trees with small traces, trace\par
stitching has a noticeable cost. Although writing to memory and\par
then soon reading back would be expected to have a high L1\par
cache hit rate, for small traces the increased instruction count has\par
a noticeable cost. Also, if the writes and reads are very close\par
in the dynamic instruction stream, we have found that current\par
x86 processors often incur penalties of 6 cycles or more (e.g., if\par
the instructions use different base registers with equal values, the\par
processor may not be able to detect that the addresses are the same\par
right away).\par
The alternate solution is to recompile an entire trace tree, thus\par
achieving inter-trace register allocation (10). The disadvantage is\par
that tree recompilation takes time quadratic in the number of traces.\par
We believe that the cost of recompiling a trace tree every time\par
a branch is added would be prohibitive. That problem might be\par
mitigated by recompiling only at certain points, or only for very\par
hot, stable trees.\par
In the future, multicore hardware is expected to be common,\par
making background tree recompilation attractive. In a closely re-\par
lated project (13) background recompilation yielded speedups of\par
up to 1.25x on benchmarks with many branch traces. We plan to\par
apply this technique to TraceMonkey as future work.\par
6.3  Trace Recording\par
The job of the trace recorder is to emit LIR with identical semantics\par
to the currently running interpreter bytecode trace. A good imple-\par
mentation should have low impact on non-tracing interpreter per-\par
formance and a convenient way for implementers to maintain se-\par
mantic equivalence.\par
In our implementation, the only direct modification to the inter-\par
preter is a call to the trace monitor at loop edges. In our benchmark\par
results (see Figure 12) the total time spent in the monitor (for all\par
activities) is usually less than 5%, so we consider the interpreter\par
impact requirement met. Incrementing the loop hit counter is ex-\par
pensive because it requires us to look up the loop in the trace cache,\par
but we have tuned our loops to become hot and trace very quickly\par
(on the second iteration). The hit counter implementation could be\par
improved, which might give us a small increase in overall perfor-\par
mance, as well as more flexibility with tuning hotness thresholds.\par
Once a loop is blacklisted we never call into the trace monitor for\par
that loop (see Section 3.3).\par
Recording is activated by a pointer swap that sets the inter-\par
preter’s dispatch table to call a single “interrupt” routine for ev-\par
ery bytecode. The interrupt routine first calls a bytecode-specific\par
recording routine. Then, it turns off recording if necessary (e.g.,\par
the trace ended). Finally, it jumps to the standard interpreter byte-\par
code implementation. Some bytecodes have effects on the type map\par
that cannot be predicted before executing the bytecode (e.g., call-\par
ingString.charCodeAt, which returns an integer orNaNif the\par
index argument is out of range). For these, we arrange for the inter-\par
preter to call into the recorder again after executing the bytecode.\par
Since such hooks are relatively rare, we embed them directly into\par
the interpreter, with an additional runtime check to see whether a\par
recorder is currently active.\par
While separating the interpreter from the recorder reduces indi-\par
vidual code complexity, it also requires careful implementation and\par
extensive testing to achieve semantic equivalence.\par
In some cases achieving this equivalence is difficult since Spi-\par
derMonkey follows afat-bytecodedesign, which was found to be\par
beneficial to pure interpreter performance.\par
In fat-bytecode designs, individual bytecodes can implement\par
complex processing (e.g., thegetpropbytecode, which imple-\par
ments full JavaScript property value access, including special cases\par
for cached and dense array access).\par
Fat bytecodes have two advantages: fewer bytecodes means\par
lower dispatch cost, and bigger bytecode implementations give the\par
compiler more opportunities to optimize the interpreter.\par
Fat bytecodes are a problem for TraceMonkey because they\par
require the recorder to reimplement the same special case logic\par
in the same way. Also, the advantages are reduced because (a)\par
dispatch costs are eliminated entirely in compiled traces, (b) the\par
traces contain only one special case, not the interpreter’s large\par
chunk of code, and (c) TraceMonkey spends less time running the\par
base interpreter.\par
One way we have mitigated these problems is by implementing\par
certain complex bytecodes in the recorder as sequences of simple\par
bytecodes. Expressing the original semantics this way is not too dif-\par
ficult, and recording simple bytecodes is much easier. This enables\par
us to retain the advantages of fat bytecodes while avoiding some of\par
their problems for trace recording. This is particularly effective for\par
fat bytecodes that recurse back into the interpreter, for example to\par
convert an object into a primitive value by invoking a well-known\par
method on the object, since it lets us inline this function call.\par
It is important to note that we split fat opcodes into thinner op-\par
codes only during recording. When running purely interpretatively\par
(i.e. code that has been blacklisted), the interpreter directly and ef-\par
ficiently executes the fat opcodes.\par
6.4  Preemption\par
SpiderMonkey, like many VMs, needs to preempt the user program\par
periodically. The main reasons are to prevent infinitely looping\par
scripts from locking up the host system and to schedule GC.\par
In the interpreter, this had been implemented by setting a “pre-\par
empt now” flag that was checked on every backward jump. This\par
strategy carried over into TraceMonkey: the VM inserts a guard on\par
the preemption flag at every loop edge. We measured less than a\par
1% increase in runtime on most benchmarks for this extra guard.\par
In practice, the cost is detectable only for programs with very short\par
loops.\par
We tested and rejected a solution that avoided the guards by\par
compiling the loop edge as an unconditional jump, and patching\par
the jump target to an exit routine when preemption is required.\par
This solution can make the normal case slightly faster, but then\par
preemption becomes very slow. The implementation was also very\par
complex, especially trying to restart execution after the preemption.\par
\par
6.5  Calling External Functions\par
Like most interpreters, SpiderMonkey has a foreign function inter-\par
face (FFI) that allows it to call C builtins and host system functions\par
(e.g., web browser control and DOM access). The FFI has a stan-\par
dard signature for JS-callable functions, the key argument of which\par
is an array of boxed values. External functions called through the\par
FFI interact with the program state through an interpreter API (e.g.,\par
to read a property from an argument). There are also certain inter-\par
preter builtins that do not use the FFI, but interact with the program\par
state in the same way, such as theCallIteratorNextfunction\par
used with iterator objects. TraceMonkey must support this FFI in\par
order to speed up code that interacts with the host system inside hot\par
loops.\par
Calling external functions from TraceMonkey is potentially dif-\par
ficult because traces do not update the interpreter state until exit-\par
ing. In particular, external functions may need the call stack or the\par
global variables, but they may be out of date.\par
For the out-of-date call stack problem, we refactored some of\par
the interpreter API implementation functions to re-materialize the\par
interpreter call stack on demand.\par
We developed a C++ static analysis and annotated some inter-\par
preter functions in order to verify that the call stack is refreshed\par
at any point it needs to be used. In order to access the call stack,\par
a function must be annotated as either FORCESSTACKor RE-\par
QUIRESSTACK. These annotations are also required in order to call\par
REQUIRESSTACKfunctions, which are presumed to access the call\par
stack transitively. FORCESSTACKis a trusted annotation, applied\par
to only 5 functions, that means the function refreshes the call stack.\par
REQUIRESSTACKis an untrusted annotation that means the func-\par
tion may only be called if the call stack has already been refreshed.\par
Similarly, we detect when host functions attempt to directly\par
read or write global variables, and force the currently running trace\par
to side exit. This is necessary since we cache and unbox global\par
variables into the activation record during trace execution.\par
Since both call-stack access and global variable access are\par
rarely performed by host functions, performance is not significantly\par
affected by these safety mechanisms.\par
Another problem is that external functions can reenter the inter-\par
preter by calling scripts, which in turn again might want to access\par
the call stack or global variables. To address this problem, we made\par
the VM set a flag whenever the interpreter is reentered while a com-\par
piled trace is running.\par
Every call to an external function then checks this flag and exits\par
the trace immediately after returning from the external function call\par
if it is set. There are many external functions that seldom or never\par
reenter, and they can be called without problem, and will cause\par
trace exit only if necessary.\par
The FFI’s boxed value array requirement has a performance\par
cost, so we defined a new FFI that allows C functions to be an-\par
notated with their argument types so that the tracer can call them\par
directly, without unnecessary argument conversions.\par
Currently, we do not support calling native property get and set\par
override functions or DOM functions directly from trace. Support\par
is planned future work.\par
