7.  Evaluation\par
We evaluated our JavaScript tracing implementation using Sun-\par
Spider, the industry standard JavaScript benchmark suite. SunSpi-\par
der consists of 26 short-running (less than 250ms, average 26ms)\par
JavaScript programs. This is in stark contrast to benchmark suites\par
such as SpecJVM98 (3) used to evaluate desktop and server Java\par
VMs. Many programs in those benchmarks use large data sets and\par
execute for minutes. The SunSpider programs carry out a variety of\par
tasks, primarily 3d rendering, bit-bashing, cryptographic encoding,\par
math kernels, and string processing.\par
All experiments were performed on a MacBook Pro with 2.2\par
GHz Core 2 processor and 2 GB RAM running MacOS 10.5.\par
Benchmark results.The main question is whether programs\par
run faster with tracing. For this, we ran the standard SunSpider test\par
driver, which starts a JavaScript interpreter, loads and runs each\par
program once for warmup, then loads and runs each program 10\par
times and reports the average time taken by each. We ran 4 differ-\par
ent configurations for comparison: (a) SpiderMonkey, the baseline\par
interpreter, (b) TraceMonkey, (d) SquirrelFish Extreme (SFX), the\par
call-threaded JavaScript interpreter used in Apple’s WebKit, and\par
(e) V8, the method-compiling JavaScript VM from Google.\par
Figure 10 shows the relative speedups achieved by tracing, SFX,\par
and V8 against the baseline (SpiderMonkey). Tracing achieves the\par
best speedups in integer-heavy benchmarks, up to the 25x speedup\par
onbitops-bitwise-and.\par
TraceMonkey is the fastest VM on 9 of the 26 benchmarks\par
