Using similar computations, we find that trace recording takes\par
about 3800 cycles per bytecode, and compilation 3150 cycles per\par
bytecode. Hence, during recording and compiling the VM runs at\par
1/200 the speed of the interpreter. Because it costs 6950 cycles to\par
compile a bytecode, and we save 26 cycles each time that code is\par
run natively, we break even after running a trace 270 times.\par
The other VMs we compared with achieve an overall speedup\par
of 3.0x relative to our baseline interpreter. Our estimated native\par
code speedup of 3.9x is significantly better. This suggests that\par
our compilation techniques can generate more efficient native code\par
than any other current JavaScript VM.\par
These estimates also indicate that our startup performance could\par
be substantially better if we improved the speed of trace recording\par
and compilation. The estimated 200x slowdown for recording and\par
compilation is very rough, and may be influenced by startup factors\par
in the interpreter (e.g., caches that have not warmed up yet during\par
recording). One observation supporting this conjecture is that in\par
the tracer, interpreted bytecodes take about 180 cycles to run. Still,\par
recording and compilation are clearly both expensive, and a better\par
implementation, possibly including redesign of the LIR abstract\par
syntax or encoding, would improve startup performance.\par
Our performance results confirm that type specialization using\par
trace trees substantially improves performance. We are able to\par
outperform the fastest available JavaScript compiler (V8) and the\par
fastest available JavaScript inline threaded interpreter (SFX) on 9\par
of 26 benchmarks.\par
8.  Related Work\par
Trace optimization for dynamic languages.The closest area of\par
related work is on applying trace optimization to type-specialize\par
dynamic languages. Existing work shares the idea of generating\par
type-specialized code speculatively with guards along interpreter\par
traces.\par
To our knowledge, Rigo’s Psyco (16) is the only published\par
type-specializing trace compiler for a dynamic language (Python).\par
Psyco does not attempt to identify hot loops or inline function calls.\par
Instead, Psyco transforms loops to mutual recursion before running\par
and traces all operations.\par
Pall’s LuaJIT is a Lua VM in development that uses trace com-\par
pilation ideas. (1). There are no publications on LuaJIT but the cre-\par
ator has told us that LuaJIT has a similar design to our system, but\par
will use a less aggressive type speculation (e.g., using a floating-\par
point representation for all number values) and does not generate\par
nested traces for nested loops.\par
General trace optimization.General trace optimization has\par
a longer history that has treated mostly native code and typed\par
languages like Java. Thus, these systems have focused less on type\par
specialization and more on other optimizations.\par
Dynamo (7) by Bala et al, introduced native code tracing as a\par
replacement for profile-guided optimization (PGO). A major goal\par
was to perform PGO online so that the profile was specific to\par
the current execution. Dynamo used loop headers as candidate hot\par
traces, but did not try to create loop traces specifically.\par
Trace trees were originally proposed by Gal et al. (11) in the\par
context of Java, a statically typed language. Their trace trees ac-\par
tually inlined parts of outer loops within the inner loops (because\par
