YETI, from Zaleski et al. (19) applied Dynamo-style tracing\par
to Java in order to achieve inlining, indirect jump elimination,\par
and other optimizations. Their primary focus was on designing an\par
interpreter that could easily be gradually re-engineered as a tracing\par
VM.\par
Suganuma et al. (18) described region-based compilation (RBC),\par
a relative of tracing. A region is an subprogram worth optimizing\par
that can include subsets of any number of methods. Thus, the com-\par
piler has more flexibility and can potentially generate better code,\par
but the profiling and compilation systems are correspondingly more\par
complex.\par
Type specialization for dynamic languages.Dynamic lan-\par
guage implementors have long recognized the importance of type\par
specialization for performance. Most previous work has focused on\par
methods instead of traces.\par
Chambers et. al (9) pioneered the idea of compiling multiple\par
versions of a procedure specialized for the input types in the lan-\par
guage Self. In one implementation, they generated a specialized\par
method online each time a method was called with new input types.\par
In another, they used an offline whole-program static analysis to\par
infer input types and constant receiver types at call sites. Interest-\par
ingly, the two techniques produced nearly the same performance.\par
Salib (17) designed a type inference algorithm for Python based\par
on the Cartesian Product Algorithm and used the results to special-\par
ize on types and translate the program to C++.\par
McCloskey (14) has work in progress based on a language-\par
independent type inference that is used to generate efficient C\par
implementations of JavaScript and Python programs.\par
Native code generation by interpreters.The traditional inter-\par
preter design is a virtual machine that directly executes ASTs or\par
machine-code-like bytecodes. Researchers have shown how to gen-\par
erate native code with nearly the same structure but better perfor-\par
mance.\par
Call threading, also known as context threading (8), compiles\par
methods by generating a native call instruction to an interpreter\par
method for each interpreter bytecode. A call-return pair has been\par
shown to be a potentially much more efficient dispatch mechanism\par
than the indirect jumps used in standard bytecode interpreters.\par
Inline threading (15) copies chunks of interpreter native code\par
which implement the required bytecodes into a native code cache,\par
thus acting as a simple per-method JIT compiler that eliminates the\par
dispatch overhead.\par
Neither call threading nor inline threading perform type special-\par
ization.\par
Apple’s SquirrelFish Extreme (5) is a JavaScript implementa-\par
tion based on call threading with selective inline threading. Com-\par
bined with efficient interpreter engineering, these threading tech-\par
niques have given SFX excellent performance on the standard Sun-\par
Spider benchmarks.\par
Google’s V8 is a JavaScript implementation primarily based\par
on inline threading, with call threading only for very complex\par
operations.\par
9.  Conclusions\par
This paper described how to run dynamic languages efficiently by\par
recording hot traces and generating type-specialized native code.\par
Our technique focuses on aggressively inlined loops, and for each\par
loop, it generates a tree of native code traces representing the\par
paths and value types through the loop observed at run time. We\par
explained how to identify loop nesting relationships and generate\par
nested traces in order to avoid excessive code duplication due\par
to the many paths through a loop nest. We described our type\par
specialization algorithm. We also described our trace compiler,\par
which translates a trace from an intermediate representation to\par
optimized native code in two linear passes.\par
Our experimental results show that in practice loops typically\par
are entered with only a few different combinations of value types\par
of variables. Thus, a small number of traces per loop is sufficient\par
to run a program efficiently. Our experiments also show that on\par
programs amenable to tracing, we achieve speedups of 2x to 20x.\par
10.  Future Work\par
Work is underway in a number of areas to further improve the\par
performance of our trace-based JavaScript compiler. We currently\par
do not trace across recursive function calls, but plan to add the\par
support for this capability in the near term. We are also exploring\par
adoption of the existing work on tree recompilation in the context\par
of the presented dynamic compiler in order to minimize JIT pause\par
times and obtain the best of both worlds, fast tree stitching as well\par
as the improved code quality due to tree recompilation.\par
We also plan on adding support for tracing across regular ex-\par
pression substitutions using lambda functions, function applica-\par
tions and expression evaluation usingeval. All these language\par
constructs are currently executed via interpretation, which limits\par
our performance for applications that use those features.\par
